# OpenCart standards

The authoritative entry point is `AGENTS.md` at the repository root — read it, plus the relevant pack in
`opencart-core/`, before writing or reviewing OpenCart code.

This file exists only so Cursor picks the standards up automatically.

Condensed rules:

- Admin authorization is two-tier. The route permission key is `parts[0]/parts[1]` (plus `parts[2]` for
  the `extension/*` list), so the method name is never part of the key, and the router enforces only
  `access`. Every write path must check `modify` itself, and the check must dominate the write.
- `install()` / `uninstall()` must check `modify` on `extension/extension/module` (or
  `extension/extension/theme`) — a direct route call bypasses core's own check.
- Schema changes run from `install()`/`uninstall()`, never from `index()`/`form()` or any read path.
- `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` does not add columns to an existing table; guard `ALTER` inside `install()`.
- Never change `<code>` in `install.xml` — the row is keyed by `code`, so a change duplicates the
  modification instead of replacing it.
- Theme `include`/`extends` use the full template path with an explicit `.twig` extension.
- Every raw query uses `DB_PREFIX` and `$this->db->escape()`; numeric ids are cast with `(int)`.
- Static gates prove syntax and structure, never behavior; a gate that has never failed has not been
  shown to work.
- Tag factual claims `[verified]`, `[baseline]`, `[exists]` or `[needs-verification]`, and verify against
  the real project before relying on anything here.
